The engine in include/optimaru/ is a C++ reimplementation, with
modifications, of the Timefold Solver core: its architecture, package and
directory layout, class names, API and local-search algorithms are derived
from the Java originals.

The domain models, constraint definitions and demo data of the following use
cases are C++ and Python translations, with modifications, of the
corresponding examples in Timefold Quickstarts
(https://github.com/TimefoldAI/timefold-quickstarts), licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0. Timefold Quickstarts is a derivative work of
OptaPlanner Quickstarts, Copyright Red Hat, Inc., affiliates, and
contributors.

  use-cases/bed-allocation
  use-cases/conference-scheduling
  use-cases/employee-scheduling
  use-cases/facility-location
  use-cases/flight-crew-scheduling
  use-cases/food-packaging
  use-cases/maintenance-scheduling
  use-cases/meeting-scheduling
  use-cases/order-picking
  use-cases/project-job-scheduling
  use-cases/school-timetabling
  use-cases/sports-league-scheduling
  use-cases/task-assigning
  use-cases/tournament-scheduling
  use-cases/vehicle-routing

use-cases/pickup-delivery extends the vehicle-routing translation above and
is likewise a derivative of the Timefold Quickstarts vehicle-routing example.

The following benchmark harnesses transcribe the corresponding quickstart's
demo data generator or constraint scoring and are derivatives of the same
examples:

  benchmarks/bed-allocation
  benchmarks/facility-location
  benchmarks/flight-crew-scheduling
  benchmarks/project-job-scheduling
  benchmarks/school-timetabling
  benchmarks/tournament-scheduling

benchmarks/pickup-delivery and the shared runner benchmarks/cpsat-common are
original to optimaru; the java.util.Random replica in the latter follows the
algorithm documented in the Java API specification, not Timefold code.

